  "narrative": "All estimates and intervals are signed to the registered hypothesis direction, so positive signed evidence supports the claim and negative signed evidence opposes it. The direct adenocarcinoma subtype robustness tests are clear. H30 gives SMARCA4-loss versus SMARCA4-intact +0.778 [0.701, 0.854] in the claimed stronger-dependency direction; H31 gives TP53-loss versus TP53-intact +0.414 [0.301, 0.527] in the claimed weaker-dependency direction; H32 gives KEAP1-loss versus KEAP1-intact +0.814 [0.734, 0.894] in the claimed weaker-dependency direction. In each case the point estimate and interval lower bound are well above the 0.15-unit threshold. I accept these three direct subtype tests and close them as within-dataset associations showing that the three core predictors retain clinically substantive associations in the dominant adenocarcinoma subtype, without implying external generalizability or causality.\n\nThe PTEN co-alteration results are mixed but informative. H66 directly estimates PTEN loss among KEAP1-loss samples in the claimed stronger-dependency direction and gives +0.378 [0.193, 0.562]. Its lower bound exceeds the threshold, so I accept that specific context: among KEAP1-loss lines in this dataset, PTEN-loss samples have substantially more negative composite dependency scores than PTEN-intact lines. I keep H66 active because I request independent validation of this context-specific claim; if validation is supportive, this becomes a well-confirmed subtype/co-alteration context, while if validation is inconclusive or contradictory the specific context claim can be downgraded without threatening the accepted overall PTEN association. H81 asks whether the PTEN association is concentrated in adenocarcinoma relative to its subtype complement. The signed estimate is -0.069 [-0.293, 0.155], opposite the claimed concentration and far below the threshold in point magnitude. I reject and close this as evidence against a meaningful adenocarcinoma-specific concentration of the PTEN effect. This is consistent with the already accepted overall PTEN result and with the clean-background PTEN contexts; PTEN loss appears not to be confined to the subtype interaction as specified.\n\nThe TP53-loss PTEN context tests remain imprecise. H79 gives a direct PTEN-loss effect among TP53-loss/KEAP1-intact samples of +0.122 [-0.214, 0.458]. The point estimate is below the threshold and the interval includes zero, near-zero effects, and effects well above the threshold, with only 39 exposed observations. I leave H79 unresolved and active because the TP53-loss background is scientifically important, but I do not claim a meaningful PTEN effect or no effect. H80 is the refined PTEN-by-TP53 interaction restricted to KEAP1-intact samples, claimed in the attenuation direction. It gives +0.133 [-0.214, 0.480], below the threshold and extremely imprecise. This is suggestive at most of attenuation, but it does not support a scientifically meaningful interaction on current evidence; I leave it unresolved and active as an open context question. H65 gives direct PTEN loss among SMARCA4-loss samples of +0.132 [-0.060, 0.323]. This is below threshold and imprecise. I leave it unresolved but defer it because the corresponding interaction H72 already rejected meaningful attenuation and the direct estimate is too small to justify higher priority.\n\nThe four new broad alteration tests do not support stronger-dependency claims. ERBB2 amplification H85 gives +0.018 [-0.094, 0.131]; APC mutation H86 gives -0.030 [-0.114, 0.054]; SMAD4 loss H87 gives +0.036 [-0.073, 0.146]; NF1 loss H88 gives +0.002 [-0.098, 0.103]. All are below the 0.15-unit threshold, and several oppose their claimed directions. I reject and close them. Overall, the new evidence strengthens the core predictor set in the dominant subtype, rejects broad ERBB2/APC/SMAD4/NF1 effects, and identifies PTEN-in-KEAP1-loss as a potentially meaningful context-specific result worth independent validation. All conclusions remain associations within the supplied lung-lineage composite-dependency dataset, not causal or externally generalizable claims.",
  "research_notes": "Round 6 appraise. Accepted direct adenocarcinoma robustness for core predictors: SMARCA4-loss stronger dependency H30, TP53-loss weaker dependency H31, KEAP1-loss weaker dependency H32. Accepted PTEN-loss stronger dependency specifically among KEAP1-loss samples H66; requested independent validation of H66 to test context specificity. Rejected PTEN effect concentration in adenocarcinoma H81. PTEN-in-TP53-loss contexts remain unresolved: H79 direct +0.122 [-0.214,0.458] underpowered; H80 refined KEAP1-intact TP53 interaction +0.133 [-0.214,0.480] imprecise attenuation. PTEN-in-SMARCA4-loss H65 +0.132 [-0.060,0.323] unresolved/deferred. New broad alterations ERBB2 H85, APC H86, SMAD4 H87, NF1 H88 rejected/closed. Current supported core: SMARCA4 loss stronger dependency; TP53 loss weaker dependency overall and in SMARCA4-intact; KEAP1 loss weaker dependency overall and in clean/KY/adenocarcinoma contexts; PTEN loss stronger dependency overall with validation, plus accepted clean/background contexts and KEAP1-loss context pending validation. Remaining uncertainty: PTEN effect modification by TP53/SMARCA4 contexts, non-Avana PTEN, and possibly KEAP1-by-TP53/high-serum interactions. All remain within-dataset associations, not causal or externally generalizable.",
